hairston@henry.ECE.CMU.EDU (David Hairston) writes:
>[merlyn@digibd.com (Merlyn LeRoy) writes:]
>[] I think I've mentioned this before: the addition of the wand of
>[] everything changes polypiling.  The WOE zaps as a random wand each
>[] time you use it; since it is a polymorphous item, it isn't affected
>[] by polymorph.

>umm, two comments and two questions:

>a wand of everything sounds like fun ... is it?

Pretty fun (in a nasty sort of way); it surprises the player
when the wand they think is fire (because it zapped fire the
first time) zaps as haste when they meet that dragon...
Also useful if you are REALLY desperate.

>couldn't you achieve the same effect with a polymorphous wand of
>nothing? (which is, unfortunately, a more severe penalty)

By polymorphous, I mean it already is polymorphous in a sense;
other examples are: chameleon (are they affected by polymorph?),
book, wand & ring of polymorph, ring of poly. control.

Possibly wands and rings of polymorph (and maybe books) shouldn't
be affected by polymorph zaps.  This would leave a number of
dead ends for items.  Adding ring of polymorph control would help, too.

I think this method would have more logic to it than booby-prize
items that stop polymorphing.

